{
  "id": "sat-functions",
  "chapterNumber": "8",
  "title": {
    "en": "Functions &amp; Function Notation",
    "ar": null
  },
  "chapterTag": {
    "variant": "category",
    "className": "sat-domain-tag",
    "emoji": "📌",
    "categoryPrefix": "Domain:",
    "category": "Advanced Math",
    "topics": [
      "Nonlinear functions",
      "Equivalent expressions",
      "Piecewise and absolute value functions"
    ]
  },
  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Evaluating, interpreting and transforming functions runs through the Advanced Math domain.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Functions on the SAT",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Evaluate \\( f(a) \\) by substitution and read features (intercepts, vertex) from graphs. \\( f(x)+k \\) shifts the graph vertically; \\( f(x-h) \\) shifts it horizontally.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Evaluate",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(a) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Composite",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(g(x)) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Shift up",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(x)+k \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Shift right",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(x-h) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 8.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>If \\( f(x)=x^{2}-1 \\), find \\( f(4) \\).</p>\\[ 16-1=15. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Piecewise &amp; Absolute Value Functions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "A <strong>piecewise function</strong> uses a different rule on each part of its domain. To evaluate, find which condition \\(x\\) satisfies, then apply only that piece. The <strong>absolute value function</strong> is itself piecewise: \\(f(x)=|x|=x\\) when \\(x\\geq0\\) and \\(-x\\) when \\(x&lt;0\\). Its graph is a V-shape with vertex where the expression equals zero.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Piecewise eval",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Match \\(x\\) to the right interval",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "\\(|ax+b|\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "V-shape; vertex at \\(x=-b/a\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Continuity",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Do pieces meet at boundary?",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "\\(|f(x)|=k\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(f(x)=k\\) or \\(f(x)=-k\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 8.B — Piecewise function",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>\\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}x^{2} &amp; x&lt;2 \\\\ 3x-1 &amp; x\\geq2\\end{cases}\\). Find \\(f(-1)\\) and \\(f(4)\\).</p><p>\\(f(-1)\\): use \\(x^2\\). \\((-1)^2=\\mathbf{1}\\).</p><p>\\(f(4)\\): use \\(3x-1\\). \\(3(4)-1=\\mathbf{11}\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Evaluating a piecewise function",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "satPieceCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "A piecewise function graph, x squared on the left piece and 3x minus 1 on the right piece, with a movable evaluation point",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "satPieceDataBtn",
          "panelId": "satPieceDataPanel",
          "descId": "satPieceDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "satPieceDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "f(x) sampled across both pieces",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            { "en": "x", "ar": null },
            { "en": "f(x)", "ar": null },
            { "en": "active piece", "ar": null }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "satPieceX",
            "label": { "en": "evaluate at <b>x</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": -4, "max": 6, "value": -1, "step": 0.5,
            "valueDisplayId": "satPieceXval",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "x-value to evaluate", "ar": null }
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "satPiecePiece",
            "label": { "en": "active piece", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "x²"
          },
          {
            "id": "satPieceF",
            "label": { "en": "f(x)", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "indigo",
            "value": "1.000"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "This is exactly Worked example 8.B's f(x): x² for x &lt; 2 (indigo), 3x − 1 for x ≥ 2 (amber). Drag x to −1 or 4 to reproduce the worked example's f(−1) = 1 and f(4) = 11, then drag across x = 2 to see the two pieces don't actually meet there.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "<strong>SAT-style:</strong> \\(g(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+5&amp;x\\leq-1 \\\\ x^{2}-3&amp;x&gt;-1\\end{cases}\\). What is \\(g(-1)+g(3)\\)?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(g(-1)=2(-1)+5=3\\). \\(g(3)=9-3=6\\). Sum \\(=\\mathbf{9}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "quizWidget": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
